<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.14.2: Interpreting a Functional Relationship between Two Quantities</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01990</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.14.2: Interpreting a Functional Relationship between Two Quantities</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d23f6368-e2a8-4852-ae86-20724b1c5a65</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Student Activity</title>
<para>The equation \(h(t)=2+23.7t-4.9t^2\) represents the height of a pumpkin that is catapulted up in the air as a function of time, \(t\), in seconds. The height is measured in meters above ground. The pumpkin is shot up at a vertical velocity of 23.7 meters per second.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What do you think the 2 in the equation \(h(t)=2+23.7t-4.9t^2\) tells us in this situation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The 2 tells us the pumpkin is 2 meters above ground before it is launched.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What do you think the \(-4.9t^2\) in the equation \(h(t)=2+23.7t-4.9t^2\) tells us in this situation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The \(-4.9t^2\) shows the height lost due to gravity (like the \(-16t^2\) in the cannonball problem).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="e5d44b58-99e2-451e-b75a-172d2b6ce390" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="6f798351-5fda-4770-a356-0e5c6e02f088" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="Downward" data-solution-options="[&quot;Upward&quot;, &quot;Downward&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>If we graph the equation, will the graph open upward or downward? Why?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph would open downward. The height starts at 2 meters and then increases as the pumpkin is shot up, and eventually, it decreases as the pumpkin falls back to the ground</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="50df570b-594f-4d04-a344-33f6fec10b26" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2">
    <problem>
      <para>Think about where the vertical intercept would be. What is the \(y\)-value of that vertical intercept?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here<emphasis>:</emphasis><emphasis/></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The vertical intercept would be at \((0,2)\), because when \(t\) is 0, the height is 2 meters.</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What about the horizontal intercepts?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The horizontal intercepts are hard to tell without graphing or having the equation in factored form. One of them will be located around \((5,0)\) because substituting 5 for \(t\) gives a height of -2 meters, which is pretty close to 0.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation \(h(t)=2+23.7t-4.9t^2\).</item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem> 
    <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="DESCRIPTION: Graph of parabola"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6f7adef0b71c401befa40b5dd8b586170d09649f"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Now that you have graphed the equation, answer questions a–c using the graph.</para>
    <para> What is the vertical intercept and what does this point mean in our catapulted pumpkin situation? </para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The vertical intercept is \((0,2)\). It tells us that the pumpkin's initial position is 2 meters above ground.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What are the horizontal intercepts and what do these points mean in our catapulted pumpkin situation? </para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The graph intersects the horizontal axis around -0.08 and 4.9. The positive horizontal intercept tells us that the pumpkin hits the ground after about 4.9 seconds. The negative intercept doesn't have any meaning here since the time cannot be negative in this case.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the vertex of the parabola and what does this point mean in our catapulted pumpkin situation? </para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The vertex is at approximately \((2.4,30.7)\). It tells us that the pumpkin reaches its maximum height of about 30.7 meters around 2.4 seconds after being fired.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
